Z||000011||LZeitgeist||04-04-2001||06:05 PM||||I've got some fluorescent red self-adhesive vinyl left over... I'll be glad to sell enough to do the inside of the instrument and gauge pods for $10, postage included, if anyone's interested...<P>Just cut pieces to fit and stick them inside the areas where the instruments sit. Pieces don't have to be cut exactly - they can overlap and it will make no difference. <P>The vinyl lasts much longer and is brighter than paint. That's why I did mine.<P>I cut the big sections out with scissors and trimmed them with a fresh X-Acto blade. If you're skilled enough to get the instruments out, you're skilled enough to do this job.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>EDIT - e-mail me at LZeitgeist@aol.com if you're interested. MONEY ORDERS ONLY. Personal checks will be returned. (Not gonna deal with any more bounced checks... nope.)<p>[This message has been edited by LZeitgeist (edited 04-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||stsmithgt||04-04-2001||06:55 PM||||I was thinking "cheap"...(ouch, thinking hurts)  There is probably a sheet of vinyl with an adhiesive back in several different colors made for covering cabinet shelves.  It seems like it would be durable if it could handle wear and tear in a cabinet unless the adhiesive doesn't last. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  Not knowing how hard it is to install, it might not be worth taking the chance on cheap.  Hey! you could get plad, stripes or flowers! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <I>sorry, that's plain sick. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> </I>||||reg||1
Z||000013||FieroLisa||04-04-2001||07:11 PM||||hmm I still want mine to light up purple...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Formula88||04-05-2001||12:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stsmithgt:<BR><B>I was thinking "cheap"...(ouch, thinking hurts)  There is probably a sheet of vinyl with an adhiesive back in several different colors made for covering cabinet shelves.  It seems like it would be durable if it could handle wear and tear in a cabinet unless the adhiesive doesn't last.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">  Not knowing how hard it is to install, it might not be worth taking the chance on cheap.  Hey! you could get plad, stripes or flowers!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> <I>sorry, that's plain sick.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> </I></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, don't forget it has to take the heat from all the lights in the dash.  I wouldn't use shelf vinyl.  I got my vinyl from LZeitgeist, and it's the same stuff he used.  I'm very happy with it.  I tried to take some pics, but with flash their too washed out to see and without it's too dark to see.  May try again this weekend if we can play with the settings.  (It's LZ's Mavica, so I'm no expert at using it.)  Also, this is flourescent vinyl, the kind used to make reflective signs with.  It'll reflect the light better than a plain colored backing.  If you didn't want to go with vinyl, you could spray paint it, but you're results wouldn't be as good, I don't think.  And in theory, you can do any color you want that you can get the vinyl in.  <P>Lisa, the purple may be too dark to see very well, but since it's easily reversible, why not give it a shot.  Maybe LZ can hook you up with some Violet vinyl!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||LZeitgeist||04-05-2001||07:38 AM||||Close, but not exactly...<P>Road signs are make with *reflective* vinyl... the vinyl is impregnated with very small glass beads that refract and reflect the light. That's not the case here.<P>Thus is *fluorescent* vinyl - the type of stuff that would glow if hit with a black light. However, due to the brightness of the color, it also reflects white light very well, tinting it with the same color.<P>Lisa... back when you were working on the dash in you previous car, I tried the different colors of fluorescent vinyl, and I discovered that there is a thin layer of reddish-orange inside the instruments (I tried fluorescent green, figuring the gauges would change to green, right? Nope... sort of an ugly, muddled brown color. &lt;bleah&gt; ). So, theoretically, all we'd have to do is line your instrument cluster with fluorescent blue, right? Maybe so, but there's no such thing as actual fluorescent blue vinyl, since it would be a color that would react to black light (which is actually a very intense bluish-purple), and if the vinyl was blue and the light was blue, they wouldn't react with each other - they'd sort of wash each other out. There *is* a bright 'neon'-type blue vinyl that the manufacturer *calls* fluorescent blue, but it is not actually fluorescent in nature, and so it wouldn't tint the light anywhere near as much as the actual fluorescent colors. I tried every color of blue vinyl (including the glass bead 'refelctive' stuff), and none of them had the right properties to change the red to purple.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000016||LZeitgeist||04-05-2001||07:43 AM||||"cheap"? What other mod or upgrade or 'fix' can you do to your Fiero for $10?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Heck, the fluorescent spraypaints I've seen (the two-part ones with the white undercoat and the tinted color) run $7-8, and they don't last very long, and it makes a huge mess to use...<P>S'up to ya... my feelings aren't hurt.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Songman||04-05-2001||08:25 AM||||Does anyone make blacklight bulbs for auto applications? Then you could just line it with reflective white and get the blacklight glow...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000018||LZeitgeist||04-05-2001||08:34 AM||||Ooooooo... *that* would be *wicked*!!!||||reg||1
Z||000019||Mr.Frosty||04-05-2001||04:00 PM||||I just did that last night.I did this before on my 1st fiero '84.The early ones are screwy.The metal panel is what dtermines the color.The light reflects off of it from clear bulbs then around the front of the gauges to produce the effect.If only someone out there could make white gauge faces for the early models the colors would be bright at night.I painted my panel bright white,and am going to use colored auto bulbs to change the color to my tastes.That'll be easier than changing the plate every 6 months.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||10
Z||000020||fyerstarter||04-05-2001||04:09 PM||||You may want to consider painting the panel with chrome spray paint. This amplifies white light but I'm not sure what it would do with colors. Also don't paint anything that will come in contact with electricity with it. The metallic flakes in the paint  are conductive.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Mr.Frosty||04-05-2001||05:43 PM||||I thought about that,but I figured the light would be uneven and Funky.I don't know i'll run some tests and come back with conclusions(looking real serious and scientific)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||9
Z||000022||Mr.Frosty||04-06-2001||12:04 PM||||Finally got my instrument cluster and console back together last night,and it looks wicked.I left the white bulbs in because of time constraints,but it is bright white now at night.All I have to do now is go to the auto store and buy the blue bilbs to match my stereo face.Now all I have to do is take off the back cover and change them.It's a lot easier than the initial teardown of the panel,which I dont want to do ever again.Now I'm going to change the heater control panel.This is a lot easier.I had a guy at work shoot a film positive on clear acetate film.It's crisp and the white areas are clear and the black areas are really black.It's done,I have to use a light white primer coating on the back,then use a flourescent color on top of that and apply the film in place of the original.I need to cut out the small holes first though.I already tested it and it works great.I'll post pics when I get my camera.The only thing left would be the glowing symbols on the trunk release and the window defog.<P>------------------<BR>88 Formula T-top <BR>85 GT Turbo||||reg||10
